, col): You give me an R at (3,2)  \n(row, col): You give me a G at (3,3)  \n\nIf you accept this, please confirm or specify alternate tiles/colors if you prefer. If not, make a new offer, but I will not give away my chips for free unless it results in a strict benefit to my score. \n, \nplayer 0: Let’s evaluate your request in detail:\n\n**You need:**  \n- (3, 2): R (from me)  \n- (3, 3): G (from me)  \n\n**I need:**  \n- (0, 1): B (from you)  \n- (1, 0): B (from you)  \n\nMy inventory: B:0, G:2, R:14  \nTo reach my own goal via the optimal path, I require 2 B, but only 1 G and 3 R, which I have.\n\nIf I give you (3,2): R, it costs me 1 R.  \nIf I give you (3,3): G, it costs me 1 G, leaving me 1 G chip.\n\n**What do I gain?**  \nIf I accept, you pay for both blue moves I can’t otherwise cover.  \nIf I refuse, I am stuck and cannot reach my own goal, since I have zero B. That’s catastrophic for my payoff (0 points without contract).\n\n**Does this deal strictly benefit me?**  \nIf I accept this contract, I get to my goal (max score) and lose 1 G, 1 R (to you), but keep all other chips (maximizing my chip count).\n\nIf I decline, I cannot get to my goal at all.\n\nTherefore: **This is advantageous, since otherwise I score zero. I accept.**\n\n**I agree to your contract:**\n\n(row, col): I give you a B at (0,1)  \n(row, col): I give you a B at (1,0)  \n(row, col): You give me an R at (3,2)  \n(row, col): You give me a G at (3,3)  \n\nagree \n, \nplayer 1: Turn: 2: agree \n",
